WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.768
Welcome to episode three.

00:00:01.808 --> 00:00:05.385
Last time we saw where
reaction energy comes from.

00:00:05.425 --> 00:00:08.423
Today we'll draw it, as an energy profile,

00:00:08.463 --> 00:00:12.619
and learn how to read and adjust
thermochemical equations,

00:00:12.659 --> 00:00:17.506
which are the equations that come
with an enthalpy change attached.

00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:19.152
Two quick checks.

00:00:19.192 --> 00:00:23.518
First: does breaking a bond
absorb energy or release it?

00:00:23.558 --> 00:00:28.352
Second: if the products have less
enthalpy than the reactants,

00:00:28.392 --> 00:00:30.847
is delta H positive or negative?

00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:32.602
Pause and answer both.

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.483
Breaking a bond absorbs energy.

00:00:43.523 --> 00:00:47.523
And if the products are
lower in enthalpy, delta H,

00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:53.703
which is products minus reactants, is
negative, so the reaction is exothermic.

00:00:53.743 --> 00:00:58.338
Hold on to both ideas; we're
about to see them on a graph.

00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:03.192
An energy profile has enthalpy
on the vertical axis.

00:01:03.232 --> 00:01:08.891
The horizontal axis is called the reaction
coordinate, or reaction progress.

00:01:08.931 --> 00:01:10.016
It is not time.

00:01:10.056 --> 00:01:16.015
It shows how far the atoms have got in
rearranging from reactants into products,

00:01:16.055 --> 00:01:20.329
so a profile says nothing
about how fast a reaction goes.

00:01:20.666 --> 00:01:24.044
Start every profile
with the two endpoints.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:27.860
Draw a flat level for the
reactants on the left,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.595
and a flat level for the
products on the right.

00:01:31.635 --> 00:01:35.689
For an exothermic reaction,
the products sit lower.

00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:40.098
The vertical gap between
the two levels is delta H.

00:01:40.138 --> 00:01:44.968
Measured from reactants down to
products, it's negative here.

00:01:45.008 --> 00:01:50.077
Notice that delta H only depends
on where the two endpoints sit.

00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:53.350
We haven't drawn anything in between yet,

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:57.062
and we don't need to in
order to know delta H.

00:01:57.999 --> 00:02:00.701
Now join the endpoints with a curve.

00:02:00.741 --> 00:02:03.291
To get from reactants to products,

00:02:03.331 --> 00:02:07.329
bonds must start breaking
before new ones fully form,

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:12.509
so the energy first rises to a
maximum, called the transition state,

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:14.910
and then falls to the products.

00:02:15.265 --> 00:02:20.943
The activation energy is the minimum energy
needed to reach the transition state

00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:22.301
from the reactants.

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:28.018
On the graph, it's the vertical distance
from the reactant level up to the peak.

00:02:28.058 --> 00:02:30.702
That's the forward activation energy.

00:02:31.065 --> 00:02:34.910
Run the reaction backwards,
from products to reactants,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.936
and you climb from the product
level up to the same peak.

00:02:38.976 --> 00:02:41.549
That's the reverse activation energy.

00:02:41.589 --> 00:02:45.786
For an exothermic reaction it's
bigger than the forward one,

00:02:45.826 --> 00:02:48.157
because the products start lower.

00:02:48.532 --> 00:02:51.219
So there are three vertical distances,

00:02:51.259 --> 00:02:55.381
and every one of them is a
difference between two heights.

00:02:55.421 --> 00:02:58.826
Forward activation energy:
peak minus reactants.

00:02:58.866 --> 00:03:02.199
Reverse activation energy:
peak minus products.

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:04.639
Delta H: products minus reactants.

00:03:04.679 --> 00:03:07.765
None of them is read
straight off the axis.

00:03:08.132 --> 00:03:11.517
For an endothermic reaction
the picture flips.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:16.059
The products sit above the
reactants, so delta H is positive,

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:20.492
and the forward activation
energy is the larger of the two.

00:03:21.464 --> 00:03:27.691
A catalyst provides an alternative reaction
pathway with a lower activation energy.

00:03:27.731 --> 00:03:31.919
On the profile, that's a second
curve with a lower peak.

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:35.659
Let's draw it as a dashed
line over the original.

00:03:35.997 --> 00:03:37.564
Look at the endpoints.

00:03:37.604 --> 00:03:43.188
The catalysed path starts at the same
reactant level and finishes at the same

00:03:43.228 --> 00:03:44.211
product level.

00:03:44.251 --> 00:03:50.273
The catalyst is not consumed, and it doesn't
change what the reactants and products

00:03:50.313 --> 00:03:53.089
are, so their enthalpies don't change.

00:03:53.464 --> 00:03:57.376
So the catalyst lowers the
forward activation energy,

00:03:57.416 --> 00:04:01.328
and the reverse activation
energy by the same amount.

00:04:01.368 --> 00:04:03.755
But delta H is exactly the same.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:04.909
Checkpoint.

00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:11.085
A student says: "Adding a catalyst makes
an exothermic reaction release more heat."

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:12.126
Is that right?

00:04:12.166 --> 00:04:14.548
Pause and decide, with a reason.

00:04:22.997 --> 00:04:23.188
No.

00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:28.952
For the same amount reacting, the heat
released equals the size of delta H,

00:04:28.992 --> 00:04:32.026
and the catalyst doesn't change delta H.

00:04:32.066 --> 00:04:35.561
The reaction may reach
the same result faster,

00:04:35.601 --> 00:04:39.675
but the energy released for
that amount is unchanged.

00:04:40.663 --> 00:04:44.917
Here's practice question Q
zero six, worth five marks.

00:04:44.957 --> 00:04:48.058
Pause the video and try all five parts.

00:05:00.463 --> 00:05:03.737
Let's draw the profile
to scale from the data.

00:05:03.777 --> 00:05:06.978
Reactants at eighty,
products at twenty-five,

00:05:07.018 --> 00:05:10.292
the uncatalysed peak at
one hundred and fifty,

00:05:10.332 --> 00:05:13.605
and the catalysed peak
at one hundred and ten.

00:05:13.645 --> 00:05:19.624
All in kilojoules per mole of reaction,
measured from the same arbitrary reference.

00:05:19.996 --> 00:05:23.628
The forward activation energy
is peak minus reactants:

00:05:23.668 --> 00:05:28.632
one hundred and fifty minus eighty,
which is seventy kilojoules per mole.

00:05:28.996 --> 00:05:32.446
The reverse activation energy
is peak minus products:

00:05:32.486 --> 00:05:35.080
one hundred and fifty minus twenty-five,

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.874
which is one hundred and
twenty-five kilojoules per mole.

00:05:39.263 --> 00:05:43.166
Delta H is products minus reactants:
twenty-five minus eighty,

00:05:43.206 --> 00:05:46.282
which is negative fifty-five
kilojoules per mole.

00:05:46.322 --> 00:05:51.155
It's negative, and the products are
lower, so that's consistent: exothermic.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:57.041
The catalysed forward activation energy is
the catalysed peak minus the reactants:

00:05:57.081 --> 00:06:01.821
one hundred and ten minus eighty,
which is thirty kilojoules per mole.

00:06:02.196 --> 00:06:07.612
And the explanation: the catalyst does
not change the heat released by the same

00:06:07.652 --> 00:06:13.136
amount reacting, because the reactant and
product levels, and therefore delta H,

00:06:13.176 --> 00:06:14.143
are unchanged.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:16.246
One mark for each part.

00:06:16.286 --> 00:06:21.210
The most common error is reading
the peak, one hundred and fifty,

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.119
as the activation energy.

00:06:23.159 --> 00:06:27.549
That's a height above an
arbitrary zero, not a difference.

00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:32.629
The first wrong step is forgetting
to subtract the reactant level.

00:06:32.996 --> 00:06:36.667
Prediction: what is the catalysed
reverse activation energy?

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:48.373
The catalysed peak minus the products:
one hundred and ten minus twenty-five,

00:06:48.413 --> 00:06:51.186
which is eighty-five kilojoules per mole.

00:06:51.226 --> 00:06:54.686
It's forty less than the
uncatalysed reverse value,

00:06:54.726 --> 00:06:58.980
the same forty that the catalyst
took off the forward barrier.

00:06:59.929 --> 00:07:05.180
A thermochemical equation is a balanced
equation with a delta H attached.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.442
Here's hydrogen burning
to form liquid water:

00:07:08.482 --> 00:07:13.081
delta H equals negative five
hundred and seventy-two kilojoules.

00:07:13.121 --> 00:07:16.342
That value refers to
the equation as written:

00:07:16.382 --> 00:07:22.285
two moles of hydrogen reacting with one
mole of oxygen to form two moles of liquid

00:07:22.325 --> 00:07:23.089
water.

00:07:23.129 --> 00:07:28.886
If you double the equation, you double the
amounts reacting, so you double delta H.

00:07:28.926 --> 00:07:33.146
Four moles of hydrogen burning
releases twice as much energy:

00:07:33.186 --> 00:07:38.454
delta H becomes negative one thousand
one hundred and forty-four kilojoules.

00:07:38.494 --> 00:07:42.056
The coefficients and delta
H always scale together.

00:07:42.396 --> 00:07:47.746
If you reverse the equation, the same
energy change runs the other way,

00:07:47.786 --> 00:07:49.113
so the sign flips.

00:07:49.153 --> 00:07:55.414
Decomposing two moles of liquid water into
hydrogen and oxygen has delta H equal to

00:07:55.454 --> 00:07:59.133
positive five hundred and
seventy-two kilojoules.

00:07:59.173 --> 00:08:04.867
It takes as much energy to pull water
apart as was released when it formed.

00:08:05.263 --> 00:08:09.062
Sometimes you want a molar
quantity for one substance.

00:08:09.102 --> 00:08:14.891
Dividing the equation by two gives one mole
of hydrogen forming one mole of water,

00:08:14.931 --> 00:08:19.797
with delta H equal to negative two
hundred and eighty-six kilojoules.

00:08:19.837 --> 00:08:25.128
So the molar enthalpy of combustion of
hydrogen is negative two hundred and

00:08:25.168 --> 00:08:28.185
eighty-six kilojoules
per mole of hydrogen.

00:08:28.225 --> 00:08:31.638
Always say which substance
"per mole" refers to.

00:08:32.596 --> 00:08:36.182
Look closely at the
states in that equation.

00:08:36.222 --> 00:08:37.995
The water is a liquid.

00:08:38.035 --> 00:08:41.826
What if the product were
water vapour instead?

00:08:42.196 --> 00:08:45.392
Turning liquid water into
vapour absorbs energy:

00:08:45.432 --> 00:08:49.437
about forty-four kilojoules per
mole at twenty-five degrees.

00:08:49.477 --> 00:08:54.736
So gaseous water sits higher in enthalpy
than the same amount of liquid water.

00:08:55.129 --> 00:09:00.682
That means forming two moles of water vapour
releases less energy than forming two

00:09:00.722 --> 00:09:06.069
moles of liquid water: about four hundred
and eighty-four kilojoules instead of

00:09:06.109 --> 00:09:08.047
five hundred and seventy-two.

00:09:08.087 --> 00:09:13.707
You can't reuse negative five hundred and
seventy-two if the product state changes.

00:09:13.747 --> 00:09:16.953
You need data for the
states actually involved.

00:09:17.329 --> 00:09:22.066
This is why states are never decoration
in a thermochemical equation.

00:09:22.106 --> 00:09:24.765
Hot exhaust gases contain water vapour,

00:09:24.805 --> 00:09:29.819
while a calorimeter at room temperature
mostly ends up with liquid water.

00:09:29.859 --> 00:09:32.766
Which value applies depends on the states.

00:09:33.729 --> 00:09:38.004
Practice question Q zero five,
worth five marks, puts scaling,

00:09:38.044 --> 00:09:40.091
reversing and states together.

00:09:40.131 --> 00:09:43.193
Pause the video and
attempt all three parts.

00:09:58.596 --> 00:09:59.076
Part a.

00:09:59.116 --> 00:10:05.091
Decomposing one mole of liquid water means
reversing the equation and halving it.

00:10:05.131 --> 00:10:08.879
Reversing flips the sign;
halving halves the value.

00:10:08.919 --> 00:10:13.780
So liquid water forms hydrogen gas
plus half a mole of oxygen gas,

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.944
with delta H equal to positive two
hundred and eighty-six kilojoules.

00:10:19.329 --> 00:10:19.763
Part b.

00:10:19.803 --> 00:10:24.769
The negative five hundred and seventy-two
refers to two moles of hydrogen,

00:10:24.809 --> 00:10:29.572
so one mole of hydrogen releases two
hundred and eighty-six kilojoules.

00:10:29.612 --> 00:10:34.848
Zero point seven five zero moles therefore
releases zero point seven five zero

00:10:34.888 --> 00:10:37.081
times two hundred and eighty-six,

00:10:37.121 --> 00:10:40.869
which is two hundred and
fourteen point five kilojoules,

00:10:40.909 --> 00:10:44.697
reported as two hundred and
fifteen kilojoules released.

00:10:45.063 --> 00:10:45.549
Part c.

00:10:45.589 --> 00:10:45.775
No.

00:10:45.815 --> 00:10:51.495
If the product were water vapour, the
products would sit higher in enthalpy,

00:10:51.535 --> 00:10:54.205
because vaporisation absorbs energy.

00:10:54.245 --> 00:11:00.265
So less energy would be released, and a
different delta H value would be needed.

00:11:00.663 --> 00:11:05.264
Marks: two for the reversed,
halved equation with its enthalpy;

00:11:05.304 --> 00:11:09.611
two for the energy released;
one for the state explanation.

00:11:09.651 --> 00:11:15.506
The tempting error is multiplying zero
point seven five zero by five hundred and

00:11:15.546 --> 00:11:19.927
seventy-two, giving four hundred
and twenty-nine kilojoules.

00:11:19.967 --> 00:11:24.937
That treats the equation's value as
if it were per mole of hydrogen.

00:11:24.977 --> 00:11:30.061
The first wrong step is not asking
what amount the delta H refers to.

00:11:30.996 --> 00:11:31.586
To recap.

00:11:31.626 --> 00:11:35.922
On an energy profile, every
quantity is a vertical difference:

00:11:35.962 --> 00:11:40.468
forward activation energy, reverse
activation energy and delta H.

00:11:40.508 --> 00:11:45.854
A catalyst lowers the peak but leaves
both endpoints, and delta H, unchanged.

00:11:45.894 --> 00:11:51.659
In a thermochemical equation, delta H scales
with the coefficients and changes sign

00:11:51.699 --> 00:11:55.896
when the equation is reversed,
and it depends on the states.

00:11:56.263 --> 00:11:57.297
Closing recall.

00:11:57.337 --> 00:12:01.952
An equation has delta H equal to
negative one hundred kilojoules.

00:12:01.992 --> 00:12:07.148
What is delta H for the reverse equation
with every coefficient tripled?

00:12:17.596 --> 00:12:19.852
Reverse it: positive one hundred.

00:12:19.892 --> 00:12:22.983
Triple it: positive
three hundred kilojoules.

00:12:23.023 --> 00:12:28.549
Next episode, we step back from equations
to the fuels themselves: fossil fuels,

00:12:28.589 --> 00:12:30.676
biofuels and the carbon cycle.
